<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#northern-thai-language">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northern Thai language</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kham Mueang, Lanna language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Northern Thai, known to its speakers as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> Mueang, is the Tai language of the former kingdom of Lan Na, spoken by several million people in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It was traditionally written in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a> used for Buddhist texts, and it remains a strong marker of northern identity, though most speakers now also use standard Thai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/northern-thai-language.jpg" alt="Northern Thai language" width="320" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Autoisme · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lanna_alphabet_and_Thai_alphabet.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Lanna, also called Kam Mueang, Tai Yuan or Northern Thai, is a Southwestern Tai language spoken by the Lanna people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It has about six million speakers, most of them in northern Thailand, with a smaller community in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. Its speakers generally regard the name Tai Yuan as pejorative and call themselves Khon Mueang, Lanna or Northern Thai. Kam Mueang means city language, and Central Thai speakers call it phasa thin phayap, the speech of the north-western region.</p><p>Most linguists classify it in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a> branch with Central Thai and Southern Thai, and consider it closer to Central Thai than to Lao, though it has been heavily influenced by both. Some modern scholars question this grouping, arguing that it may reflect political Thaification and that Lanna has closer historical ties with the Lao-Phutai branch. The language has six tones in the Chiang Mai dialect. Its verbs do not inflect, and word order is subject, verb, object.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestors of the Lanna people spoke Southwestern Tai dialects and migrated from what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Small-scale movement into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> took place mainly between the eighth and twelfth centuries, and the ancestors of the Lanna followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>. They founded the early kingdom of Ngoenyang and smaller states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> beside the Mon kingdom of Hariphunchai. The Mon writing system was eventually adapted for the language as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-tham-script">Tai Tham script</a>.</p><p>In the 13th century King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> consolidated these lands as the kingdom of Lan Na. Under King Tilokkarat in the 15th century a golden age of Northern Thai literature produced many palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> in Tai Tham, written in the vernacular and mixed with Pali and Buddhist Indic vocabulary.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the Burmese were driven out in 1775, the northern principalities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of Siam, which annexed them in 1899. The Compulsory Education Act of 1921 banned schools and temples from using any language other than Central Thai, and Khruba Srivichai and other religious leaders were jailed for preaching in Lanna. Thai cultural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> of the 1940s reinforced the prestige of Central Thai.</p><p>Today Lanna is often mixed with standard Thai in urban areas, while remoter areas use it alone. The traditional script is largely limited to Buddhist temples, where old sermon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> are still used, and Thai script is used for writing the language, though it cannot show all of its tones. There is a renewed interest in writing it in the traditional way.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lanna language” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Northern Thai identity, including the <b>Kham Mueang</b> language, still draws on Lan Na's history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
